"The word hal in this verse expresses affirmation; it has the meaning of \"came\" or \"drew near.\" This conveys in definitive terms that the creation of man occurred after the creation of the universe. Subsequently, it is stated that man was created to be tested, and that the grateful will attain salvation, while the ungrateful will suffer punishment.\n\nThe human species was created after the beginning of time. Dahr is the span of time that elapses from the existence of the universe until its end. Hin, on the other hand, is an unknown duration of time within this span prior to the creation of man. During that period, man was not something mentioned; that is, man in today's sense did not exist.\n\nMan was created from a mixed drop of fluid (nutfah). Nutfah is the pure seed, which is a drop of semen. The word amshaj means \"mixed\"; it has been interpreted either as a single mixture or as a combination of various mixtures. According to Ibn Mas'ud, they are the vessels of the nutfah, and according to Qatadah, its colors and states. This demonstrates that man underwent a gradual and complex creation, rather than a simple one.\n\nMan was created to be tested and was made hearing and seeing. Allah showed him the right way; choosing this way and being grateful, or being ungrateful and denying, was left to his own choice. This guidance was realized through rational and transmitted proofs supported by the verses of the Quran and the signs in the universe.\n\nChains, shackles, and a blazing fire have been prepared for the disbelievers. The righteous (abrar), however, will drink in the Hereafter from a cup whose mixture is camphor (kafur). The mixture of this cup is camphor; this is either the name of one of the fountains of Paradise or a characteristic of the drink. Unlike worldly wine, this drink does not cause intoxication; it provides complete joy and peace.\n\nThe expression 'camphor' in the sixth verse is mentioned as an additive to the drink of Paradise, and it is stated that this additive is a spring. This spring is a source from which the 'servants of Allah' drink and cause to gush forth wherever they wish. Some commentators have said that this source has gold pipes. In the seventh verse, it is stated that the good servants 'fulfill their vows.' Nadhr is a good deed that a person makes obligatory upon himself; this verse emphasizes that they consistently fulfill Allah's commandments and the promises they made to themselves.\n\nIn the eighth verse, it is narrated that these good servants 'feed food out of love for it.' Even if they are in need themselves, they give food to the needy, the orphan, and the captive. Special attention is drawn to treating captives well. In the ninth verse, it is stated that they do this feeding solely for the sake of Allah and expect no reward. In the tenth verse, it is expressed that they fear a grim and distressful day, that is, the terror of the Day of Resurrection.\n\nIn the eleventh and twelfth verses, glad tidings are given that Allah will protect them from the evil of that Day and bestow radiance upon their faces and joy upon their hearts. In reward for their patience, they will be given Paradise and garments of silk. In the thirteenth and fourteenth verses, they are depicted as reclining upon couches, seeing neither excessive heat nor bitter cold.\n\nIn the fifteenth and sixteenth verses, crystal vessels and cups of silver are mentioned. These vessels are a unique depiction combining the whiteness of silver with the clarity of crystal. In the seventeenth verse, another cup whose mixture is ginger is offered. In the eighteenth verse, a spring named 'Salsabil' is mentioned; this word denotes water that flows smoothly and is easy to drink.\n\nIn the nineteenth verse, it is stated that immortal youths circulate around them. In the twentieth verse, it is explained that wherever one looks in Paradise, the eye sees great bliss and a vast realm. In the twenty-first verse, it is expressed that upon them are garments of fine green silk and brocade, that they are adorned with bracelets of silver, and that their Lord gives them a pure drink. This drink is interpreted as a spiritual drink that purifies from malice and impurity.\n\nIn the twenty-second verse, the people of Paradise are addressed: 'Indeed, this is a reward for you, and your striving has been appreciated.' Subsequently, in the twenty-third verse, it is recalled that the Quran was revealed gradually in stages. In the twenty-fourth verse, the Messenger of Allah is commanded to be patient and not to obey the sinners and the ungrateful. In the twenty-fifth and twenty-sixth verses, he is asked to remember Allah morning and evening, to prostrate at night, and to perform the tahajjud prayer.\n\nIn the twenty-seventh verse, it is stated that the disbelievers love this fleeting world and leave a heavy Day behind their backs. In the twenty-eighth verse, it is expressed that Allah created human beings and bound their joints firmly, and that whenever He wills, He will replace them with others like them. The twenty-ninth verse states: 'This is indeed a reminder; so whoever wills, let him take a path to his Lord.' In the thirtieth verse, it is emphasized that human will is dependent upon Allah's will by stating: 'And you cannot will except as Allah wills.' In the thirty-first verse, it is declared that Allah admits whomever He wills into His mercy, and that for the wrongdoers He has prepared a painful punishment."